    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 4 tests, 0 failures).\n\n## Summary\n\nThe spec requires that a new owner joining an existing household has their `membershipLevel` capped to at most one above the current maximum among their household members, with no cap when the household is empty. The existing code hard-rejected any second household member with a 409, which is why the new acceptance test failed \u2014 so that rejection had to give way to the softer cap the spec introduces.\n\nChanges, each kept in the unit responsible for it:\n\n- **`MembershipLevelCalculator`** (domain logic): added `effectiveMembershipLevel(owner)` \u2014 the computed level lowered by the owner's stored cap \u2014 and `householdLevelCeiling(members)` \u2014 one above the highest effective level among members.\n- **`Owner`** (entity): added a `membershipLevelCap` field (column `membership_level_cap`), a snapshot recorded at creation, alongside the other creation-time snapshots (`namesakeCount`, `householdSize`, \u2026), plus getter/setter and `toString`.\n- **`OwnerRestControllerV1.applyHouseholdRule`**: instead of throwing `HouseholdDuplicateException`, a new owner joining an existing household is now created with its cap set to the household ceiling; the `sharesHousehold` flag still governs possible-duplicate flagging (declared members aren't flagged; undeclared ones run the usual detection). Removed the now-unused import.\n- **`OwnerMapper`** and **`OwnerAuditLogger`**: now report the *effective* (capped) level.\n- **Schema files** (h2/hsqldb/mysql/postgres) and the **OpenAPI** `membershipLevel` description updated to match.\n\nThe cap is computed from each member's *effective* level, so caps compose correctly down a growing household. In the test, owner B's natural level (2) equals A's level (1) + 1, so both are created and the ceiling holds.",
